00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
 The next interesting metal that we're going to meet this week are alloy steels.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:08,160
 alloy steels are pretty similar to the plain carbon steels that we met a few videos ago.

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:11,760
 The key difference being that whereas plain carbon steel is typically derived

4
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:16,160
 their nice mechanical properties by the addition of more and more carbon to be able to form

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:21,280
 martensite, alloy steels derive their nice mechanical properties by precipitation hardening or

6
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,720
 interstitial alloying. And it's not just mechanical properties either.

7
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:28,720
 By incorporating substantial amounts of all sorts of different alloying elements you can

8
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:34,160
 significantly change the strength and the ductility but also corrosion resistance and

9
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:39,840
 electrical conductivity and thermal conductivity and melting point and many other properties of

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:44,240
 alloy steels. On the textbook page for this video I've included a table of common alloy

11
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,160
 elements and the reasons that they're usually in their the effects that they have on material

12
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:52,880
 properties. And I won't get into too much detail here because the family of alloy steels is

13
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:57,760
 as wide and diverse as the field of alloying itself. Again on the textbook page attached to this

14
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:02,000
 video there are a few examples that you may want to check out. One that I will talk about,

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,320
 a very very common alloy steel that you're likely to run into is called chromoly. Chromoly is a

16
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:13,280
 medium carbon alloy steel that has as the name suggests substantial amounts of chromium and

17
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:18,000
 malibdenum added. In this alloy the addition of chromium normally aids with corrosion resistance

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,840
 for reasons that we'll talk about a little bit more in the next video actually when we get to

19
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:26,080
 stainless steels. And malibdenum which significantly improves the strength and the hardness of the

20
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:31,360
 alloy steel. Chromoly comes in a variety of grains that vary in their percentage addition of these

21
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:36,080
 alloy elements but also in the percentage addition of any carbon that's added. In fact you can get

22
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:41,440
 some varieties of chromoly that only have as much carbon as a low carbon steel but a significantly

23
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:45,760
 better mechanical properties significantly better strength. A very common grade of chromoly

24
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:50,320
 called 4130 chromoly that you're likely to encounter as an engineer because it can be quite

25
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:55,040
 easily worked and welded and machined and otherwise used to manufacture components.

26
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:59,360
 Is relatively easily accessible and falls into this camp. It has about as much carbon as a

27
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:05,040
 low carbon steel but significantly higher strength compared to the 250 to 350 you might get in a

28
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:09,520
 low carbon steel. Chromoly is typically used in places where you want a high strength or a higher

29
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:14,800
 hardness but still want to maintain a decent amount of ductility. For example in framework structures

30
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:20,480
 like aircrafts and bicycle tubes and notably our Formula SAE chassis. My in-you-racey students

31
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:24,640
 who design and build race car frames every year have been using chromoly for quite a long time.

32
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:28,640
 Partly because the supplier who tube cuts these frames a process we'll talk about next week

33
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:32,960
 are only supplies in chromoly but also because the strength to weight ratio of the chromoly

34
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:37,520
 tubing is generally much higher than the mild steel equivalent meaning that you can build a

35
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:42,160
 chassis frame which is just as strong but is going away significantly less and objective it's

36
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:46,400
 quite important for race car builders. We'll leave that discussion on alloy steels there and I encourage

37
